Mobile hasFocus is unreliable, two ways, both washing out the titlebar/menubar
(which drive off IHostService.hasFocus via onDidChangeFocus). One: a WebView
resume restores document focus without dispatching any event the host samples
below, so the latched value wedges stale (gray titlebar, and element focus()
never raises the on-screen keyboard) - re-sampled on pointerdown and a post-
visibility poll. Two: on touch the soft keyboard's native chrome (iOS's close-
keyboard button, the keyboard surface) takes and drops window focus without the
user leaving, so document.hasFocus() reports the app inactive while it is plainly
foreground - so the hasFocus getter answers by visibility on touch (present ==
active) rather than OS window focus. Vendor-defect workaround, kept individually
deletable.

2026-07-22 re-check (prune pass): not re-verifiable here. The Composery Mobile
app WebView loads the IDE but exposes no devtools socket, so document.hasFocus()
cannot be read nor focus() injected, and the gray-titlebar signal is too subtle
on the mobile layout to judge from a screenshot; only WebView 134 is available
(current 151/152 will not run under the x86_64 emulator's ARM translation).
Retest a background/resume cycle on a physical device with a debuggable WebView
before deleting.

